  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, cool, and breezy conditions continue through \nThursday.  \n\nOver the next several days southern New England will see a \npersistence forecast without much change. Basically, cooler than \naverage temperatures under persistent northeast flow will be the \nstory of the week thanks to our placement south of a broad area of \nhigh pressure over eastern Canada. As this high pressure continues \nto sit and even expand, a coastal low pressure system will slowly be \nmeandering up the eastern seaboard. While this will likely bring \nmore direct impacts to the region at the end of the week, we'll \nbegin to feel the impacts of the ever tightening pressure gradient \nas early as Tuesday. Those breezy northeast winds will be even \nmoreso on Wednesday. While the strongest gusts will be over the \nwaters where Gale Warnings are in effect, locations along the coast \nand especially the Cape and Islands are increasingly likely to see \nwind gusts 25 to 35 mph each day through Thursday. This, while high \ntemperatures struggle to get out of the low 60s so together it will \nfeel much cooler than the normal low 70s we'd expect this time of \nyear. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Probabilities are increasing for a strong coastal \nlow pressure system to bring impacts to southern New England in the \nform of rain, rough seas, strong winds, and coastal erosion late \nweek into the weekend. \n\nThursday onward is where uncertainty ramps up considerably, owing to \nthe significant spread amongst the guidance as to how this coastal \nlow evolves in its track up the coast. That being said, the last few \nruns of GFS guidance have trended toward the ECMWF solution showing \na retrograding back to the west, meandering with little forward \nmotion through the weekend. Of course, this is only one \ndeterministic run, but it is so far a trend for a few run and GEFS \nensemble guidance has also evolved to show a stronger, more western \nlow track with the 00z run. Should this be the solution that pans \nout, there's a good chance we'll be dealing with storm force wind \ngusts on the waters and wind advisory conditions at least for \ncoastal locations by Friday. Additionally, the many days of \npersistent and increasing NE flow will lead to high seas and \npotential for significant coastal erosion on the east coast, \nespecially given astronomically high tides. It doesn't appear that \nthe low is likely to finally exit east until at least early next \nweek but again, confidence in specifics is low Friday onward.",
